[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No switch or L2/L3 fault is present. The local Ethernet segment between PC15 and PC16 is healthy and carrying traffic normally.

Evidence:
- PC15 -> PC16 ping: 0% loss, RTT ~0.43–0.98 ms.
- PC16 -> PC15 ping: 0% loss, RTT ~0.51–0.82 ms.
- Both hosts’ NICs are up with correct addressing: PC15 eth0 172.18.51.15/24, PC16 eth0 172.18.51.16/24, default via 172.18.51.254, state UP/LOWER_UP.
- These endpoints sit on the same access switch (switch10) on adjacent access ports (cd_sw10_fa1_1 and cd_sw10_fa2_1), which are clearly passing unicast ICMP and ARP.

Mechanism/why the symptom doesn’t match: If PC16’s switch port were disabled, both ARP and ICMP would fail and we would see 100% loss; instead, we have clean, symmetric reachability with sub-millisecond latency indicating a functioning L2 domain.

Localization: Access segment on switch10 between:
- PC15: switch10.eth1 (cd_sw10_fa1_1)
- PC16: switch10.eth2 (cd_sw10_fa2_1)

Root cause: There is no network-side failure; the reported “dead port” is not corroborated. Any difficulty “sharing files” is likely above the network layer (host firewall or file-sharing service configuration), not the switch.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Please verify application-layer settings on the PCs (e.g., SMB/file sharing enabled and allowed through host firewalls). The switch port for PC16 does not need to be turned on or moved; it is already working. === END ===